<article class="env-lemma" id="0GSW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GSW">Lemma <span data-tag="0GSW">15.98.8</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring and let $f \in A$ be a nonzerodivisor. Let $M^\bullet $ and $N^\bullet $ be two bounded complexes of finite free $A$-modules representing the same object in $D(A)$. Assume $I_ i(M^\bullet , f)$ is a principal ideal for all $i \in \mathbf{Z}$. Then $J_ i(M^\bullet , f) = J_ i(N^\bullet , f)$ as ideals in $A/fA$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Observe that the fact that $I_ i(M^\bullet , f)$ is a principal ideal implies that $I_ i(M^\bullet , f)$ is a principal ideal by Lemma <a href="/tag/0GSS" data-tag="0GSS">15.98.1</a> and hence the statement makes sense. As in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0GSS" data-tag="0GSS">15.98.1</a> we may assume $N^\bullet = M^\bullet \oplus Q^\bullet $ for some trivial complex $Q^\bullet $, i.e., 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  Q^\bullet = \ldots \to 0 \to A \xrightarrow {1} A \to 0 \to \ldots  \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $A$ is placed in degree $j$ and $j + 1$. Since $\eta _ f$ is compatible with direct sums, we see that the map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  (1, d^ i) : (\eta _ fN)^ i / f(\eta _ fN)^ i \longrightarrow f^ iN^ i/f^{i + 1}N^ i \oplus f^{i + 1}N^{i + 1}/f^{i + 2}N^{i + 1}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is the direct sum of the corresponding map for $M^\bullet $ and for $Q^\bullet $. By the universal property defining the ideals in question, we conclude that $J_ i(N^\bullet , f) = J_ i(M^\bullet , f) + J_ i(Q^\bullet , f)$. Hence it suffices to show that $J_ i(Q^\bullet , f) = 0$ for all $i$. This is a computation that we omit. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>